# kernel-debug-ll.config — compiled-in UART debugging, for bringing up a kernel that says nothing.
#
# DEBUG_LL puts the debug UART's address IN THE IMAGE instead of discovering it from the DTB, and
# EARLY_PRINTK exposes it as `earlyprintk`. That is the output you need when the DTB-driven `earlycon`
# never registers — or when the kernel dies before it could.
#
# Do NOT use the DEBUG_SUNXI_UART0 choice here: it means 0x01c28000, the A10/A20-era base. The T113 is
# an R528-family die whose UART0 is at 0x02500000 (snps,dw-apb-uart, per the board DTB), so that choice
# sends every byte to a peripheral that does not exist — silence that looks exactly like a dead kernel.
# Name the address explicitly via the generic 8250 path; dw-apb registers are 32-bit, hence SHIFT=2.
CONFIG_DEBUG_KERNEL=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_LL=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_LL_UART_8250=y
CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_PHYS=0x02500000
# VIRT follows the sunxi convention, 0xf0000000 | (PHYS & 0x00ffffff) — the same shape as the in-tree
# 0x01c28000 -> 0xf1c28000. It must be a VMALLOC-range address, not PHYS: head.S maps PHYS->VIRT in
# __create_page_tables, and once paging_init replaces those tables debug_ll_io_init() re-creates the
# mapping at VIRT. Set VIRT == PHYS and printascii works until paging_init and then faults on an address
# nothing maps any more. (That buys nothing for the decompressor either: DEBUG_UNCOMPRESS depends on
# !ARCH_MULTIPLATFORM, and every modern sunxi kernel is multiplatform.)
CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_VIRT=0xf2500000
CONFIG_DEBUG_UART_8250_SHIFT=2
CONFIG_EARLY_PRINTK=y
